# rules: java-path-from-untrusted-input
# languages: java
# fixture: java/CWE-22.java
# from: CWE-22 (Path Traversal), CWE-23, CWE-73
#
# A servlet parameter is a String, and so is a path, and nothing between them
# objects. `new File(uploads, name)` resolves `../../../etc/shadow` without
# complaint and hands back a File outside the directory the program meant.
#
# The sources are the request and nothing else. A path out of a properties
# file or off the command line was chosen by whoever runs the program, which
# is a different question, and mixing the two buries this one.
#
# `getFileName()` and `FilenameUtils.getName()` are sanitizers because after
# either there is one component and no separator left, which is a fact a rule
# can check. `normalize()` and `getCanonicalPath()` are not: both resolve `..`
# rather than rejecting it, so the path they return is still outside the
# directory - it just says so tidily. A program that canonicalises and *then*
# compares is safe, and the comparison is the part that makes it safe; this
# rule cannot see it, and reports the open. That is the honest under-read: see
# "Name sanitizers honestly".
#
# Every sink is focused onto the path before the value is followed, and both
# argument positions are written out. `new File(base, name)` and
# `Paths.get(base, name)` put the interesting half second, and a reading that
# only asked about the first would have found the constant.
#
# One bug, one finding. A method that builds a path and then opens it holds
# two sinks in the same chain - `new File(base, name)` and then
# `new FileInputStream(wanted)` - and reporting both says the same thing
# twice, on the line that matters and on the line after it. So the builders
# are always reported, and an opener is reported only in a method that never
# builds a path, which is exactly the case where the request's string went
# straight into the open. The test is the method body read as text, and it
# costs the second half of a method that builds one path and opens another.
#
# jax-rs-path-traversal asks a narrower version of this question of @PathParam
# specifically. A file that reaches for HttpServletRequest inside a JAX-RS
# resource could be reported by both; the sources do not otherwise overlap.

["$R.getParameter($K)", "$R.getParameterValues($K)", "$R.getHeader($K)",
 "$R.getQueryString()", "$R.getPathInfo()", "$R.getPathTranslated()",
 "$R.getRequestURI()"] as $outside
| ["new File($P)", "new File($_, $P)",
   "Paths.get($P)", "Paths.get($_, $P)",
   "Path.of($P)", "Path.of($_, $P)",
   "$D.resolve($P)"] as $builders
| ["new FileInputStream($P)", "new FileOutputStream($P)",
   "new FileReader($P)", "new FileWriter($P)",
   "new RandomAccessFile($P, $_)",
   "Files.readAllBytes($P)", "Files.readAllLines($P)",
   "Files.newInputStream($P, $$$_)", "Files.newOutputStream($P, $$$_)",
   "Files.delete($P)", "Files.deleteIfExists($P)"] as $openers
| ["FilenameUtils.getName($X)", "$X.getFileName()"] as $clean
| ["$RT $M($$$_) { $$$BODY }"] as $methods
| "new File\\s*\\(|Paths\\.get\\s*\\(|Path\\.of\\s*\\(|\\.resolve\\s*\\(" as $assembles
| scan_ast("*.java"; $builders + $openers + $clean + $methods + $outside) as $all
| ( ($all | of($builders) | focus("P"))
    + ($all | of($openers) | focus("P")
       | outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $assembles))) )
| reaching($all | of($outside); $all | of($clean))
| finding("java-path-from-untrusted-input";
    "$P came from the request, so a caller who sends ../ reads or writes "
    + "outside the directory this was meant to stay in")
| report
